From 49aabce150fe772948630eaf51bd5267cbdd2ce0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:30:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] c11/threads_posix: detect pthread_mutex_timedlock
 instead of listing OSes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mtx_timedlock chose between pthread_mutex_timedlock and its own
trylock loop with

  #if !defined(__CYGWIN__) && !defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(__NetBSD__)
  #define EMULATED_THREADS_USE_NATIVE_TIMEDLOCK
  #endif

which is a hand-maintained list of the platforms then known to lack the
function. Whether a C library provides pthread_mutex_timedlock is not
something an OS name can answer: newlib declares it only under
_POSIX_TIMEOUTS and does not implement it at all, on any OS it is used
with.

A configure check now decides. It sits beside the existing
pthread_setaffinity_np check, uses the same form, and defines
HAVE_PTHREAD_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK. The three names removed are covered by
it: Cygwin, macOS and NetBSD do not provide the function, so the check
answers NO there and they keep the emulated path they had.

No fallback is added. The trylock loop the #else selects has always
been in the file for precisely this case.

Accepted cost: a system that provides pthread_mutex_timedlock only via
a separate -lpthread that dependency('threads') does not turn up would
now take the emulated path. That is slower, not wrong, and it is the
same exposure every other has_function check in this file carries.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 3 (newlib/libnx gaps)
Why: devkitA64's newlib has no pthread_mutex_timedlock, so Mesa's C11
  threads implementation does not compile for Horizon. It is a libc
  gap, but it surfaces in the file milestone item 4 (threads) owns —
  recorded as a deviation in STATUS.md.
Evidence: building src/c11/impl for aarch64/horizon fails with
    threads_posix.c:207:10: error: implicit declaration of function
    'pthread_mutex_timedlock'; did you mean 'pthread_mutex_trylock'?
  It is genuinely absent, not merely hidden by a feature-test macro: a
  probe that declares the prototype by hand still fails to link,
    undefined reference to `pthread_mutex_timedlock'
  and the symbol is in none of libc.a, libpthread.a or libnx.a.
  pthread.h does declare it, but only #if defined(_POSIX_TIMEOUTS),
  which is not defined here even after including <unistd.h>.
Upstream: yes — it replaces a platform blocklist with the feature test
  it stands in for, and reproduces the blocklist's answers exactly.
---
 meson.build                  | 7 +++++++
 src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c | 7 ++++++-
 2 files changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index 4cfbd6b..ddec752 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1763,6 +1763,13 @@ if dep_thread.found()
       args : '-D_GNU_SOURCE')
     pre_args += '-DHAVE_PTHREAD_SETAFFINITY'
   endif
+  if cc.has_function(
+      'pthread_mutex_timedlock',
+      dependencies : dep_thread,
+      prefix : '#include <pthread.h>',
+      args : '-D_GNU_SOURCE')
+    pre_args += '-DHAVE_PTHREAD_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK'
+  endif
 endif
 
 with_expat = get_option('expat') \
diff --git a/src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c b/src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c
index 6db8c42..1476c01 100644
--- a/src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c
+++ b/src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c
@@ -19,7 +19,12 @@ Configuration macro:
     Use pthread_mutex_timedlock() for `mtx_timedlock()'
     Otherwise use mtx_trylock() + *busy loop* emulation.
 */
-#if !defined(__CYGWIN__) && !defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(__NetBSD__)
+/* Whether pthread_mutex_timedlock exists is a property of the C library,
+ * not of the operating system. The list this replaces named the three
+ * platforms then known to lack it; a configure check covers those and
+ * every other C library that does not provide it. The mtx_trylock loop
+ * below is the fallback, and was already written for exactly this. */
+#if defined(HAVE_PTHREAD_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK)
 #define EMULATED_THREADS_USE_NATIVE_TIMEDLOCK
 #endif
 
